boeing's beleaguered 737 max airliner  will be allowed to fly again  after the faa removed a grounding order  just this morning  now the jet has not flown passengers  since march of last year  after two deadly crashes linked to a  design flaw killed 346 people  and led to the worldwide grounding of  the plane chris van cleave is at reagan  national airport  right outside washington dc with more on  the story so chris when could the 737  max return to service  well good morning gail before the plane  can take off a couple of things have to  happen  airlines have to install the updates  that address the design issues with the  plane  and then pilots have to get additional  training including simulator time  but at least one u.s carrier says it  intends to fly the 737 max with  passengers  before the end of the year  the 737 max ungrounding order comes  after nearly 20 months of work  addressing a design flaw that led to two  deadly crashes just months apart  at the center of the redesign is the  mcas anti-stall system  linked to both accidents the changes  agreed upon by regulators from around  the world  add redundancy to the sensors that can  trigger mcas while limiting how that  system operates  so it should no longer be able to  overpower a flight crew  faa administrator steve dixon flew the  plane last month  we're going to make sure that the  process is completed correctly  the ungrounding is unwelcome news for  the families of the 346  killed in the max crashes passengers  should look to fly on a different plane  than the boeing 737 max michael stumo  lost his daughter on ethiopian 302.  there is not  yet enough redundancy and modernization  of the systems  to guard against a third yet a third  crash  boeing says the plane is safe and will  now be able to resume  deliveries the max is believed to  account for approximately one-third of  boeing's revenue over the next five  years  its grounding has cost the company an  estimated 20 billion dollars  how do you feel about getting behind the  controls of the airplane  once it goes back into service i'm  looking forward to flying the max  737 captain laura einsettler when you  see the experienced pilots coming on  board with confidence and in our  training and our mastery  and you can know that it's safe american  airlines says  it intends to resume 737 max passenger  service as soon as december 29th  on a single daily route between miami  and laguardia and back to miami  of course yet to be resolved flyer  feelings about  getting back on board the 737 max  anthony clearly critical to boeing's  bottom line chris but a lot of people  may be reluctant to get on board  thank you
 

